,\\sigma(i))\\), and together they cover the whole\n   complement.  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{minimum number of tiles}=a+b.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **A permutation with \\(a=b=45\\).**  \n   Partition the rows into 45 blocks of 45 rows each.\n   In block \\(k\\;(1\\le k\\le45)\\) list the numbers decreasing from\n   \\(45k\\) to \\(45(k-1)+1\\).  Formally, for  \n\n   \\[\n   i=45(k-1)+r\\qquad(1\\le k,r\\le45)\n   \\]\n\n   define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\displaystyle \\sigma(i)=45k-r+1 } .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   This permutation consists of 45 decreasing blocks of length 45.\n   *Increasing subsequence:* at most one element can be taken from each block,\n   and the first element of every block (the numbers \\(45,90,\\dots ,2025\\))\n   gives an increasing subsequence of length 45; hence \\(\\operatorname{LIS}=45\\).\n   *Decreasing subsequence:* the first block itself is decreasing of length 45,\n   and a decreasing subsequence cannot use elements from two different blocks\n   (the numbers in later blocks are larger).  Thus \\(\\operatorname{LDS}=45\\).\n\n6. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   For the permutation (3) we have \\(a=b=45\\); by (2) the complement can be\n   tiled with exactly \\(45+45=90\\) rectangles.  The construction described in\n   the proof of (2) yields an explicit tiling: the monotone path consists of\n   45 vertical steps followed by 45 horizontal steps, producing 45 \u201cleft\u2019\u2019 rectangles\n   and 45 \u201cright\u2019\u2019 rectangles.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than 90 tiles, while the\n   explicit construction above shows that 90 tiles are sufficient.  Hence the\n   minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{90}\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  A placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the statement leaves\nexactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  Consequently the\nset of uncovered squares is\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  All other unit squares\nmust be covered by pairwise disjoint axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Notation\n\nFor a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) define  \n\n\\[\na=\\operatorname{LIS}(\\sigma)=\\max\\{|I|\\mid I\\text{ is an increasing subsequence of }\\sigma\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nb=\\operatorname{LDS}(\\sigma)=\\max\\{|D|\\mid D\\text{ is a decreasing subsequence of }\\sigma\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A universal lower bound\n\n**Lemma 2.1 (Erd\u0151s\u2013Szekeres).**  \nFor any permutation of length \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nab\\ge n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  The classical Erd\u0151s\u2013Szekeres theorem states that any sequence of\n\\(n\\) distinct numbers contains an increasing subsequence of length at least\n\\(\\lceil\\sqrt n\\rceil\\) or a decreasing subsequence of the same length.\nEquivalently, \\(ab\\ge n\\). \u220e\n\nFrom \\(ab\\ge n\\) we obtain by the arithmetic\u2013geometric mean inequality\n\n\\[\na+b\\ge2\\sqrt{ab}\\ge2\\sqrt n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025=45^{2}\\) this gives\n\n\\[\na+b\\ge2\\cdot45=90 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling of the complement of a permutation matrix of size\n\\(2025\\times2025\\) uses at least \\(90\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimal number of rectangles equals \\(a+b\\)\n\nWe now prove that for every permutation \\(\\sigma\\) the complement\n\\([n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\) can be partitioned into exactly \\(a+b\\) rectangles,\nand that no partition can use fewer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Upper bound \u2013 a construction\n\nConsider the Robinson\u2013Schensted correspondence applied to \\(\\sigma\\).\nLet \\(P\\) be the insertion tableau and let \\(\\lambda=(\\lambda_{1},\\lambda_{2},\\dots )\\)\nbe its shape (a partition).  A fundamental property of the correspondence is\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_{1}=a\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\\lambda'_{1}=b,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda'\\) is the conjugate partition (the column lengths of the\ndiagram).  Draw the Young diagram of \\(\\lambda\\) inside the \\(n\\times n\\) grid\nwith its north\u2011west corner at the origin.  Its border is a lattice path\n\\(\\Pi\\) that starts at \\((0,\\lambda_{1})\\), proceeds by \\(\\lambda_{1}\\) unit\nvertical steps, then by \\(\\lambda'_{1}\\) unit horizontal steps, and ends at\n\\((\\lambda'_{1},0)\\).  The path \\(\\Pi\\) is monotone (only down and left moves).\n\nThe cells of the grid that lie **strictly above** \\(\\Pi\\) (north\u2011west region)\nform a union of \\(\\lambda_{1}=a\\) disjoint rectangles:\nfor each vertical step of \\(\\Pi\\) take the maximal rectangle that extends\nwestwards to column\u202f0 and northwards to the previous horizontal step.\nAnalogously, the cells that lie **strictly to the right** of \\(\\Pi\\) (south\u2011east\nregion) form a union of \\(\\lambda'_{1}=b\\) disjoint rectangles,\neach obtained by extending the rectangle eastwards to column\u202f\\(n\\) and\nsouthwards to the next vertical step.\n\nBecause every point \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) of the permutation matrix lies on the\nborder \\(\\Pi\\) (this is a standard property of the Robinson\u2013Schensted\ncorrespondence), none of the \\(a+b\\) rectangles contains a point of\n\\(U\\).  Moreover the two regions together cover every cell that is not in\n\\(U\\).  Hence we have a tiling with exactly \\(a+b\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Lower bound \u2013 any tiling needs at least \\(a+b\\) rectangles\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal T\\) be an arbitrary tiling of the complement.\nBecause a tile cannot intersect a hole, for each tile \\(R\\in\\mathcal T\\) the\nwhole column interval of \\(R\\) is either **strictly left** of the hole in every\nrow covered by \\(R\\) or **strictly right** of that hole.  Accordingly we call\n\\(R\\) a *left* tile or a *right* tile.\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be the set of left tiles and \\(\\mathcal R\\) the set of right\ntiles; \\(|\\mathcal T|=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\\).\n\n*Left tiles.*  \nFor each left tile \\(L\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc(L)=\\max\\{\\,\\text{column of a cell of }L\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nr(L)=\\min\\{\\,\\text{row of a cell of }L\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause all left tiles lie to the left of the holes, we have\n\\(c(L)<\\sigma(r(L))\\) for every \\(L\\in\\mathcal L\\).\nOrder the left tiles by increasing \\(r(L)\\):\n\\(L_{1},L_{2},\\dots ,L_{|\\mathcal L|}\\) with\n\\(r(L_{1})<r(L_{2})<\\dots\\).\n\nTwo distinct left tiles cannot overlap in columns; otherwise they would\nshare a cell.  Hence\n\n\\[\nc(L_{1})<c(L_{2})<\\dots <c(L_{|\\mathcal L|}).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(c(L_{k})<\\sigma(r(L_{k}))\\) and (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(r(L_{1}))>\\sigma(r(L_{2}))>\\dots >\\sigma(r(L_{|\\mathcal L|})).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus the rows \\(r(L_{1}),\\dots ,r(L_{|\\mathcal L|})\\) together with their\n\\(\\sigma\\)-values form a decreasing subsequence of \\(\\sigma\\).\nConsequently \\(|\\mathcal L|\\ge b\\).\n\n*Right tiles.*  \nThe symmetric argument (using the leftmost column of each right tile) shows\nthat the rows of the right tiles contain an increasing subsequence, whence\n\\(|\\mathcal R|\\ge a\\).\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\\ge a+b .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Conclusion of the lemma\n\nCombining the upper bound (construction) and the lower bound (any tiling)\ngives\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{minimum number of rectangles}=a+b=\\operatorname{LIS}(\\sigma)+\n\\operatorname{LDS}(\\sigma)} .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A permutation with \\(\\operatorname{LIS}=\\operatorname{LDS}=45\\)\n\nWrite the rows as \\(i=45(k-1)+r\\) with \\(1\\le k,r\\le45\\) and define\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\sigma(i)=45k-r+1 } .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nIn words: the permutation consists of the 45 blocks\n\n\\[\n(45,44,\\dots ,1),\\;(90,89,\\dots ,46),\\;\\dots ,\\;(2025,2024,\\dots ,1981).\n\\]\n\n*Longest increasing subsequence.*  \nBecause each block is decreasing, an increasing subsequence can contain at\nmost one element from each block; there are 45 blocks, so \\(\\operatorname{LIS}\\le45\\).\nThe subsequence formed by the first element of every block,\n\\((45,90,\\dots ,2025)\\), is increasing of length 45; hence\n\\(\\operatorname{LIS}=45\\).\n\n*Longest decreasing subsequence.*  \nThe first block itself is decreasing of length 45, so \\(\\operatorname{LDS}\\ge45\\).\nIf a decreasing subsequence used an element from a later block, that element\nwould be larger than every element of an earlier block, contradicting\ndecrease.  Thus no decreasing subsequence can cross block boundaries and\n\\(\\operatorname{LDS}\\le45\\).  Hence \\(\\operatorname{LDS}=45\\).\n\nConsequently for the permutation (11)\n\n\\[\na=b=45 .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Achieving the bound for the original problem\n\nBy (10) the complement of the permutation matrix of (11) can be tiled with\nexactly \\(a+b=45+45=90\\) rectangles.  The construction described in\n\u00a73.1 gives an explicit tiling: the border path consists of 45 vertical steps\nfollowed by 45 horizontal steps; the 45 vertical strips give the \u201cleft\u2019\u2019\nrectangles, the 45 horizontal strips give the \u201cright\u2019\u2019 rectangles.  All\nrectangles are axis\u2011parallel, pairwise disjoint, avoid the uncovered squares,\nand together cover every other unit square.\n\nTogether with the universal lower bound (5) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=90 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nMatilda needs at least \\(90\\) tiles, and she can achieve the requirement\nwith exactly \\(90\\) tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{90}.\n\\]"
