ered square, $p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$ is a permutation.\n\nFor a fixed $i$ the covered cells in row $i$ are exactly the two (possibly empty) intervals  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\,\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{\\,p(i)+1,p(i)+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all covered squares is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\{i\\}\\times L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\{i\\}\\times R_i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, i.e. a set  \n\\[\n\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}\\times\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\}\n\\]\nwith $1\\le a\\le b\\le n$, $1\\le c\\le d\\le n$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – a tiling with $2n-2$ tiles\n\nFor every row $i$ with $p(i)>1$ place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand for every row $i$ with $p(i)<n$ place  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{i\\}\\times\\{p(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach $T_i^{\\text{left}}$ (resp. $T_i^{\\text{right}}$) is a $1\\times(p(i)-1)$ (resp. $1\\times(n-p(i))$) rectangle; it lies completely inside $\\mathcal C$ because it avoids the hole $(i,p(i))$.  \nThe tiles belonging to distinct rows are disjoint, and within a fixed row the left and right tiles are separated by the hole, so all tiles are pairwise disjoint.  \n\nThe number of non‑empty left tiles equals the number of rows with $p(i)>1$, which is $n-1$ (exactly one row has $p(i)=1$).  \nSimilarly, the number of non‑empty right tiles is $n-1$ (exactly one row has $p(i)=n$).  \nHence the construction uses  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles and covers every square of $\\mathcal C$.  \nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least $2n-2$ tiles are necessary\n\nWe treat the left and right sides separately.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (left side).\n\nDefine $\\ell_i=p(i)-1$; the numbers $\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n$ are a permutation of $\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}$.  \nFor each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}$ let $i_k$ be the unique index with $\\ell_{i_k}=k$; equivalently $p(i_k)=k+1$.  \nThe cell  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(i_k,k)}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nis the rightmost covered cell in row $i_k$ (the rightmost element of $L_{i_k}$).\n\nConsider any rectangle $R$ that lies entirely inside the left side $\\bigcup_i\\{i\\}\\times L_i$.  \nIf $R$ contains the cell $(i_k,k)$, then the column interval of $R$ must be a subset of $\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}$, because row $i_k$ has no covered cells in columns $>k$.  \nConsequently $R$ cannot contain any cell $(i_{k'},k')$ with $k'>k$, since those cells lie in columns $>k$.  \nThus **each rectangle can contain at most one of the distinguished cells (2).**\n\nThere are $n-1$ such cells, one for each $k=1,\\dots ,n-1$.  \nTherefore any tiling of the left side needs at least $n-1$ rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\text{left side needs }\\ge n-1\\text{ tiles}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2 (right side).\n\nDefine $r_i=n-p(i)$; the numbers $r_1,\\dots ,r_n$ also form a permutation of $\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}$.  \nFor each $k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}$ let $j_k$ be the unique index with $r_{j_k}=k$, i.e. $p(j_k)=n-k$.  \nThe cell  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(j_k,\\,n-k+1)}\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nis the leftmost covered cell in row $j_k$ (the leftmost element of $R_{j_k}$).\n\nNow take any rectangle $R$ contained in the right side $\\bigcup_i\\{i\\}\\times R_i$.  \nIf $R$ contains the cell (4), then its column interval must be a subset of $\\{\\,n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\,\\}$, because row $j_k$ has no covered cells left of column $n-k+1$.  \nHence $R$ cannot contain any cell $(j_{k'},\\,n-k'+1)$ with $k'>k$, which lies strictly left of the column interval of $R$.  \nThus **each rectangle can contain at most one of the distinguished cells (