the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i<r_j\\) (if any) and another covering the cells with \\(r_j<i\\le n\\) (if any).  All rectangles are disjoint, avoid the uncovered squares, and together cover every other cell.  The columns containing the topmost and bottommost uncovered squares contribute only one rectangle each, giving a total of \\(2n-2\\).  \n7. With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\nThus the minimum (and therefore the maximal possible) number of tiles is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix.  \nDenote by \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the bijection defined by  \n\n\\[\n\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\sigma(i)).\n\\]\n\nFor a cell \\((i,j)\\) write \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\) as \u201cto the left of the uncovered square in its row\u2019\u2019 and \\(j>\\sigma(i)\\) as \u201cto the right of it\u201d.  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\cup R\\) is precisely the set of covered unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Separation of the two regions)  \nAny axis\u2011aligned rectangle that contains a cell of \\(L\\) cannot contain a cell of \\(R\\), and vice versa.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle have row interval \\([a,b]\\) and column interval \\([c,d]\\).  \nIf it contains a cell \\((i,j)\\in L\\), then \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\).  \nIf the same rectangle also contained a cell \\((i',j')\\in R\\), then \\(j'>\\sigma(i')\\).  \nSince the rectangle\u2019s column interval is contiguous, we have \\(c\\le j\\le d\\) and \\(c\\le j'\\le d\\).  \nHence \\(c\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\le d\\) and \\(c\\le j'\\!>\\!\\sigma(i')\\ge c\\).  \nThus \\(\\sigma(i)\\in[c,d]\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')\\in[c,d]\\), contradicting the fact that the rectangle must avoid all uncovered squares. \u220e\n\nConsequently a tiling of \\(L\\cup R\\) splits into a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\); the total number of rectangles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;+\\;\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Rectangular partition of a skyline)  \nLet \\(h_i\\ge0\\) be integers for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le h_i\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(H=\\{h_i>0\\}\\) the set of distinct positive values among the \\(h_i\\).  \nThe minimum number of axis\u2011aligned rectangles that partition \\(S\\) equals \\(|H|\\).\n\n*Proof (upper bound).*  \nFor each \\(t\\in H\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_t=\\bigl\\{(i,j)\\mid h_i\\ge t,\\;1\\le j\\le t\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(h_i\\ge t\\) implies \\(\\sigma(i)=h_i+1>t\\), the rectangle \\(R_t\\) contains no uncovered square.  \nThe rectangles \\(\\{R_t\\mid t\\in H\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they occupy different column intervals) and their union is exactly \\(S\\).  Hence \\(S\\) can be partitioned with \\(|H|\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof (lower bound).*  \nConsider any rectangle \\(R\\) in a partition of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) must be contained in \\(S\\), its rightmost column is some integer \\(t\\).  \nTake a row \\(i\\) with \\(h_i\\ge t\\); the cell \\((i,t)\\) belongs to \\(S\\) and must be covered by a rectangle whose rightmost column is exactly \\(t\\) (otherwise the rectangle would extend to column \\(t+1\\) and would contain the uncovered square \\((i,h_i+1)\\)).  \nThus each distinct positive column index \\(t\\) that appears in \\(S\\) forces a distinct rectangle.  The set of such columns is precisely \\(H\\).  Hence any partition uses at least \\(|H|\\) rectangles.\n\nBoth bounds coincide, proving the claim. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Application of Lemma\u202f2 to the left region  \n\nFor the left region \\(L\\) we have \\(h_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\{\\sigma(i)-1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the set of distinct positive values among the \\(h_i\\) is \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), whose cardinality is \\(n-1\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f2, any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles and there exists a tiling with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Application of Lemma\u202f2 to the right region  \n\nFor the right region \\(R\\) the number of cells in row\u202f\\(i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nk_i=n-\\sigma(i)=\\bigl(n-(\\sigma(i)-1)\\bigr)-1.\n\\]\n\nThus the multiset \\(\\{k_i\\}\\) is also \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nExactly the same argument as for \\(L\\) shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles and can be achieved with \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board  \n\nEvery tiling of the whole board consists of a tiling of \\(L\\) together with a tiling of \\(R\\).  \nConsequently any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(r_j)=j\\) (the uncovered square in column\u202f\\(j\\)).  \n\n*Above the point.*  \nIf \\(r_j>1\\), place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nA_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<r_j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Below the point.*  \nIf \\(r_j<n\\), place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nB_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach rectangle is a single column, its interior contains no uncovered square, and rectangles belonging to different columns are disjoint.  \nThe column that contains the topmost uncovered square (\\(r_j=1\\)) contributes no \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 rectangle, and the column that contains the bottommost uncovered square (\\(r_j=n\\)) contributes no \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 rectangle.  Hence the total number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\text{#columns with }r_j>1\\bigr)+\\bigl(\\text{#columns with }r_j<n\\bigr)= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nAll covered cells are covered exactly once, and the uncovered squares are left empty.  Thus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number \\(2n-2\\).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
