s \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) let the unique uncovered unit square be at column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nDefine the two disjoint sets of covered squares  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus the set of all covered squares is \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (tiles stay in one side)  \n\nLet \\(T\\) be an axis‑parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of covered squares.  \nWrite \\(T=[i_1,i_2]\\times[c_1,c_2]\\) with integer coordinates.  \nIf there exist rows \\(r\\in[i_1,i_2]\\) such that \\(c_1\\le\\sigma(r)-1<c_2\\), then column \\(\\sigma(r)\\) lies between \\(c_1\\) and \\(c_2\\); consequently the cell \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) would belong to \\(T\\), contradicting that it is uncovered.  \nHence for every row \\(r\\) in the rectangle either \\(c_2\\le\\sigma(r)-1\\) (the rectangle lies completely to the left of the hole in that row) or \\(c_1\\ge\\sigma(r)+1\\) (the rectangle lies completely to the right).  \nSince \\(c_1,c_2\\) are the same for all rows of the rectangle, one of the two alternatives holds for **all** rows, i.e. \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (lower bound for \\(L\\))  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) is the rightmost covered cell of that row in \\(L\\).  \nThe numbers \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) are distinct because \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation; they form the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{0\\}\\).  \n\nA rectangle has a unique rightmost column. If a rectangle contained two distinct cells \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\