            "content": "**### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have solved the problem completely. The minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{22}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction.**  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: one square in every row and in every column.  \n   The problem is therefore equivalent to: *cover the complement of a permutation matrix in an \\(n\\times n\\) board (\\(n=2025\\)) by axis‑parallel rectangles that avoid the permutation points, using as few rectangles as possible*.\n\n2. **Upper bound – a recursive construction.**  \n   Split the board into two halves of rows (\\(a=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), \\(b=\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\)) and two halves of columns (the left half has \\(b\\) columns, the right half has \\(a\\) columns).  \n   Place the uncovered squares so that every top row uses a column from the right half and every bottom row uses a column from the left half; this is possible because the two halves have the same sizes.  \n   Then the two diagonal blocks (top‑left \\(a\\times b\\) and bottom‑right \\(b\\times a\\)) contain no uncovered squares and each can be covered by a single rectangle.  \n   The two off‑diagonal blocks are smaller squares of sizes \\(a\\) and \\(b\\); apply the same construction recursively.  \n   The recursion depth is \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\); at each level we use exactly two rectangles, so the total number of rectangles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=2025\\) we have \\(\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil=11\\), giving \\(2\\cdot11=22\\) tiles.\n\n3. **Lower bound – impossibility with fewer rectangles.**  \n   *Key Lemma.* If a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) avoids all uncovered squares, then  \n\n   \\[\n   |I|+|J|\\le n .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   (Otherwise a row in \\(I\\) would have its uncovered column inside \\(J\\), contradicting the avoidance.)  \n\n   Using (1) we prove by induction on \\(n\\) that any family of fewer than \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) rectangles cannot block **all** permutations; i.e. there exists a permutation matrix completely avoiding those rectangles.  \n\n   In the induction step we split the board into the four quadrants defined by the middle row and middle column.  \n   Every rectangle is “short’’ in at least one direction: either \\(|I|\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) (short in rows) or \\(|J|\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) (short in columns).  \n   Consequently a rectangle can intersect at most one of the two diagonal quadrants (top‑left or bottom‑right).  \n   Hence one of the diagonal quadrants meets at most \\(\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor\\) rectangles, where \\(t\\) is the total number of rectangles.  \n   Because \\(t<2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{t}{2}\\Big\\rfloor < 2\\Big\\lceil\\log_2\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   By the induction hypothesis there is a permutation inside that quadrant avoiding all its rectangles.  \n   The same argument applies to the opposite diagonal quadrant.  \n   Placing the two permutations in the two diagonal quadrants yields a full \\(n\\times n\\) permutation that avoids **every** rectangle of the original family.  \n\n   Therefore any covering of the complement of a permutation matrix must contain at least \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper and lower bounds coincide, so the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\lceil\\log_2 2025\\rceil = 2\\cdot11 = 22 .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; tiles are pairwise disjoint and each unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Reduction to a permutation matrix\n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid\\text{the square in row }i\\text{ and column }j\\text{ is uncovered}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one element from each row and each column.  \nHence \\(U\\) is a permutation matrix: there is a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\qquad\\text{with}\\qquad\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll other \\(n^{2}-n\\) unit squares must be covered by tiles, and a tile may not contain any point of \\(U\\).\n\nThus the problem is:\n\n> **Problem.**  For a given permutation \\(\\sigma\\) on \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), cover the set \\([n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\) by axis‑parallel rectangles that avoid \\(U\\).  Determine the smallest possible number of rectangles, minimised over all choices of \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Upper bound – a construction with \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) tiles\n\nWe construct a covering for the *identity* permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); any other permutation can be obtained from the identity by permuting rows and columns, which does not change the number of tiles needed.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\na=\\bigl\\lfloor \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad \nb=\\bigl\\lceil \\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nSplit the rows into a top block \\(R_{1}=\\{1,\\dots ,a\\}\\) and a bottom block \\(R_{2}=\\{a+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSplit the columns into a left block \\(C_{1}=\\{b+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (size \\(a\\)) and a right block \\(C_{2}=\\{1,\\dots ,b\\}\\) (size \\(b\\)).  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares as follows:\n\n* for each \\(i\\in R_{1}\\) put the uncovered square in column \\(i\\in C_{2}\\);\n* for each \\(i\\in R_{2}\\) put the uncovered square in column \\(i\\in C_{1}\\).\n\nBecause \\(|R_{1}|=|C_{2}|=a\\) and \\(|R_{2}|=|C_{1}|=b\\), this indeed yields a permutation matrix.\n\nNow observe:\n\n* The rectangle \\(T_{1}=R_{1}\\times C_{1}\\) (top‑left block) contains **no** uncovered square, because rows of \\(R_{1}\\) use only columns of \\(C_{2}\\).  Hence \\(T_{1}\\) can be taken as a tile.\n* The rectangle \\(T_{2}=R_{2}\\times C_{2}\\) (bottom‑right block) also contains no uncovered square, so it is a second tile.\n\nThe remaining uncovered squares lie in the two off‑diagonal blocks \\(R_{1}\\times C_{2}\\) and \\(R_{2}\\times C_{1}\\); each of these blocks is a smaller square board of size \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), respectively, and the uncovered squares inside each block again form a permutation matrix (the restriction of \\(\\sigma\\)).  \n\nWe therefore apply the same construction recursively to the two smaller squares.\n\nAt each recursion level we use exactly two tiles (the two diagonal blocks).  \nThe side length is at most halved at each step, so the recursion stops after  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2} n\\bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nlevels (when the side length becomes \\(1\\)).  Consequently the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2L=2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2} n\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}2025\\bigr\\rceil\n   =\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}2048\\bigr\\rceil\n   =11,\n\\]\n\nhence the construction uses \\(2\\cdot11=22\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A geometric property of a rectangle that avoids the permutation\n\nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a rectangle (with \\(I,J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) intervals) that does **not** contain any point of the permutation matrix \\(U\\).  \nIf \\(|I|+|J|>n\\) then, because \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, the set \\(\\sigma(I)\\) has \\(|I|\\) distinct columns.  Since \\(|J|>n-|I|\\), the pigeon‑hole principle forces  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(I)\\cap J\\neq\\varnothing,\n\\]\n\ni.e. some uncovered square would lie inside \\(R\\), a contradiction.  Therefore every admissible rectangle satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|I|+|J|\\le n.}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIn particular a rectangle cannot span the whole set of rows or the whole set of columns.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Lower bound – any covering needs at least \\(2\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) tiles\n\nWe prove the following statement.\n\n> **Lemma 4.1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal R\\) be a family of \\(t\\) axis‑parallel rectangles in an \\(n\\times n\\) board, each satisfying (4).  \n> If \\(t<2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) then there exists a permutation matrix \\(U\\) that avoids **all** rectangles of \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n*Base \\(n=1\\).*  The board consists of a single square; with \\(t=0\\) the empty family trivially avoids the unique permutation.\n\n*Inductive step.*  Assume the claim holds for all sizes \\(<n\\) and let \\(\\mathcal R\\) be a family of \\(t<2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) rectangles on the \\(n\\times n\\) board.\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSplit the board into the four quadrants determined by the horizontal line after row \\(m\\) and the vertical line after column \\(m\\):\ntop‑left \\(Q_{TL}\\) (rows \\(1\\!-\\!m\\), columns \\(1\\!-\\!m\\)),\ntop‑right \\(Q_{TR}\\) (rows \\(1\\!-\\!m\\), columns \\(m+1\\!-\\!n\\)),\nbottom‑left \\(Q_{BL}\\) (rows \\(m+1\\!-\\!n\\), columns \\(1\\!-\\!m\\)),\nbottom‑right \\(Q_{BR}\\) (rows \\(m+1\\!-\\!n\\), columns \\(m+1\\!-\\!n\\)).\n\nBecause of (4), each rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) is *short* in at least one direction:\n\n\\[\n|I|\\le m\\quad\\text{or}\\quad |J|\\le m .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIf a rectangle is short in rows (\\(|I|\\le m\\)), then its row interval lies completely inside either the top half or the bottom half; consequently it can intersect **at most one** of the two diagonal quadrants \\(Q_{TL}\\) and \\(Q_{BR}\\).  \nSimilarly, a rectangle short in columns can intersect at most one of the diagonal quadrants.\n\nThus every rectangle meets at most one diagonal quadrant, and the total number of rectangles meeting the two diagonal quadrants together is at most \\(t\\).  Consequently one of the diagonal quadrants, say \\(Q_{TL}\\), meets at most  \n\n\\[\nt_{1}= \\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{t}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nrectangles.  The opposite diagonal quadrant \\(Q_{BR}\\) meets at most  \n\n\\[\nt_{2}= \\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{t}{2}\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(t_{1}\\) and \\(t_{2}\\) satisfy\n\n\\[\nt_{i}<2\\Bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\rceil,\n\\qquad i=1,2,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(t<2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) and \\(\\lceil\\log_{2}\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\rceil\\le\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil-1\\).\n\nApply the induction hypothesis to the square board \\(Q_{TL}\\) of size \\(m\\times m\\) with its at most \\(t_{1}\\) intersecting rectangles.  We obtain a permutation matrix \\(U_{TL}\\) inside \\(Q_{TL}\\) that avoids all those rectangles.  \nApply the same argument to \\(Q_{BR}\\) (size \\((n-m)\\times(n-m)\\)) to obtain a permutation matrix \\(U_{BR}\\) avoiding the rectangles intersecting that quadrant.\n\nNow define a permutation matrix \\(U\\) on the whole \\(n\\times n\\) board by\n\n\\[\nU = U_{TL}\\ \\cup\\ U_{BR}.\n\\]\n\nAll uncovered squares of \\(U\\) lie in the two diagonal quadrants; any rectangle that does **not** intersect a diagonal quadrant is completely contained in one of the off‑diagonal quadrants \\(Q_{TR}\\) or \\(Q_{BL}\\) and therefore cannot contain a point of \\(U\\).  \nRectangles intersecting a diagonal quadrant have been avoided by the corresponding \\(U_{TL}\\) or \\(U_{BR}\\).  Hence **no** rectangle of \\(\\mathcal R\\) contains a point of \\(U\\).\n\nThus a permutation avoiding all rectangles exists, completing the induction. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Consequence for the tiling problem\n\nLet \\(t_{\\min}(n)\\) denote the smallest number of tiles that can cover the complement of *some* permutation matrix on an \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \nThe construction of Section 2 shows  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}(n)\\le 2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLemma 4.1 shows that any family of fewer than \\(2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) rectangles fails to avoid at least one permutation; equivalently, for every family of \\(t<2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) rectangles there exists a permutation that **cannot** be covered by those rectangles.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}(n)\\ge 2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7) and (8) we obtain the exact value\n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}(n)=2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Application to the original problem\n\nFor the given board \\(2025\\times2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}2025\\bigr\\rceil\n   =\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}2048\\bigr\\rceil\n   =11 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}(2025)=2\\cdot11=22 .\n\\]\n\nMatilda can achieve the required configuration with 22 rectangular tiles (the recursive construction above), and it is impossible with 21 or fewer tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{22}\\)."
