\\le n/2\\) or \\(|J|\\le n/2\\) (otherwise \\(|I|+|J|>n\\)).  This observation is the cornerstone of the lower\u2011bound argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Lower bound \u2013 impossibility with fewer than \\(2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) rectangles\n\nWe prove the following lemma.\n\n> **Lemma 4.1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal R\\) be a family of \\(t\\) axis\u2011parallel rectangles in an \\(n\\times n\\) board, each satisfying (4).  If  \n\n> \\[\n> t<2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil,\n> \\]\n\n> then there exists a permutation matrix \\(U\\) that avoids every rectangle of \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base cases.**  \nFor \\(n=1\\) the board consists of a single square; with \\(t=0\\) the empty family trivially avoids the unique permutation.  \nFor \\(n=2\\) we have \\(\\lceil\\log_{2}2\\rceil=1\\), so \\(t<2\\) means \\(t=0\\) or \\(t=1\\).  Any single rectangle satisfying (4) is either a single row, a single column, or a single cell; in each case one can choose a permutation (either \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) or \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) that avoids the rectangle.  Thus the lemma holds for \\(n=2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the lemma holds for all board sizes \\(<n\\) and consider a family \\(\\mathcal R\\) of \\(t<2\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil\\) rectangles on an \\(n\\times n\\) board.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nDraw the horizontal line after row \\(m\\) and the vertical line after column \\(m\\); this partitions the board into four quadrants:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ_{TL}&=\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,m\\},\\\\\nQ_{TR}&=\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\times\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\nQ_{BL}&=\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,m\\},\\\\\nQ_{BR}&=\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{m+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause each rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) satisfies (4), we have  \n\n\\[\n|I|+|J|\\le n.\n\\]\n\nIf both \\(|I|>m\\) and \\(|J