\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,i-1\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (i-1)\\) tile lying in row \\(i\\) and to the left of the hole\n\\((i,i)\\).  Again no diagonal cell belongs to this rectangle.\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{R_i^{\\text{up}}\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\n           \\{R_i^{\\text{down}}\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  Every non‑diagonal cell belongs to\nexactly one of them, and no rectangle contains a diagonal cell.  Hence the\nboard can be tiled with \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nDefine the binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nM=(m_{ij})_{1\\le i,j\\le n},\\qquad \nm_{ij}= \\begin{cases}\n0 &\\text{if }i=j,\\\\[2pt]\n1 &\\text{if }i\\neq j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe entries equal to \\(1\\) are precisely the squares that must be covered.\nA rectangular tile covering rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns\n\\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) corresponds to the rank‑1 binary matrix  \n\n\\[\nu\\,v^{\\mathsf T},\\qquad \nu_i=\\mathbf 1_{i\\in I},\\; v_j=\\mathbf 1_{j\\in J}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a tile may not contain a hole, for every index \\(k\\) we cannot have\nboth \\(u_k=1\\) and \\(v_k=1\\); otherwise the cell \\((k,k)\\) (which is a zero of\n\\(M\\)) would belong to the rectangle.  Thus each tile yields a rank‑1 matrix\n\\(u\\,v^{\\mathsf T}\\) whose support avoids all diagonal positions.\n\nA **fooling set** for a binary matrix is a set \\(S\\) of its \\(1\\)-entries such\nthat for any two distinct \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in S\\) at least one of the cross\nentries \\((a,d)\\) or \\((c,b)\\) is a \\(0\\).  The size of a fooling set is a lower\nbound on the Boolean rank (the minimum number of rank‑1 binary matrices whose\nBoolean sum equals the original matrix).  Consequently any covering of the\n\\(1\\)-entries by rank‑1 matrices, and a fortiori any tiling by rectangles,\nneeds at least \\(|S|