ix of the set of covered squares.  \nLet \\(J\\) be the \\(n\\times n\\) all\u2011ones matrix and let \\(P_{\\pi}\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\) (the matrix with a single \\(1\\) in each row and column, at positions \\((i,\\pi(i))\\)).  \nThen\n\n\\[\nA = J - P_{\\pi}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1.  \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Subtract the first row of \\(A\\) from each of the remaining rows.  \nThe resulting matrix has first row equal to \\((1,1,\\dots ,1)\\) and, for each\n\\(i\\ge2\\), the \\(i\\)-th row has a single entry \\(-1\\) in column \\(\\pi(i)\\) and zeros elsewhere.\nHence the matrix is triangular after a suitable permutation of columns,\nand its determinant equals \\((-1)^{\\,n-1}(n-1)\\neq0\\).  Therefore \\(A\\) is invertible,\nso \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nSuppose the board is tiled with \\(t\\) axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles, none of which contains an uncovered square.\nFor the \\(k\\)-th tile let  \n\n\\[\nI_k\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad J_k\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the sets of rows and columns it occupies.  \nBecause the tile is hole\u2011free, for every \\(i\\in I_k\\) we have \\(\\pi(i)\\notin J_k\\); equivalently,\n\\(\\pi(I_k)\\cap J_k=\\varnothing\\).\n\nThe indicator matrix of this tile is the outer product  \n\n\\[\nU_kV_k^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(U_k\\) (resp. \\(V_k\\)) is the column vector of length \\(n\\) whose\n\\(i\\)-th entry is \\(1\\) iff \\(i\\in I_k\\) (resp. \\(j\\)-th entry is \\(1\\) iff \\(j\\in J_k\\)).\nThus \\(U_kV_k^{\\mathsf T}\\) is a rank\u2011\\(1\\) matrix whose \\(1\\)-entries are exactly the squares of the tile.\n\nSince the tiles partition the set of covered squares, we have\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t}U_kV_k^{\\mathsf T}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nTaking ranks and using subadditivity of rank,\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(U_kV_k^{\\mathsf T})\n= \\sum_{k=1}^{t}1 = t .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma\u202f1, \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nt\\ge n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nHence any tiling must use at least \\(n\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\pi^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the unique row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(j\\).  \nPlace a vertical tile\n\n\\[\nT_j = \\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{r_j\\}\\bigr)\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the whole column \\(j\\) except the single uncovered cell \\((r_j,j)\\).\nEach \\(T_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-1)\\times1\\); the \\(n\\) tiles occupy\npairwise distinct columns, so they are disjoint.  \nTogether they cover every square that is not uncovered.\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion\n\nFrom (3) any tiling needs at least \\(n\\) rectangles, and the construction above shows that \\(n\\) rectangles suffice.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing the output, carefully review the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure:\n- All mathematical statements are precise and justified.\n- No extraneous commentary or informal remarks are present.\n- Every piece of notation is introduced before use and all formulas are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n- The lower\u2011bound argument correctly uses the rank contradiction, and the construction indeed yields a valid tiling.\n- The final answer is presented in the required \\(\\boxed{\\,\\cdot\\,}\\) format."
