ets \\(\\{i\\mid\\pi(i)>c\\}\\) shrink when \\(c\\) increases, we have \\(r_c\\ge r_{c'}\\).  \nThus \\(I\\) contains all rows from \\(r_{c'}\\) up to \\(r_c\\) and \\(J\\) contains all columns from \\(c\\) to \\(c'\\).\n\nBy the definition of \\(r_{c'}\\) it is the **largest** index with \\(\\pi(i)>c'\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(r_{c'})\\le c'.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(r_c\\ge r_{c'}\\) and \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, we must also have \\(\\pi(r_c)\\le c'\\); otherwise \\(r_c\\) would belong to the set for column \\(c'\\) and would be larger than the maximal element \\(r_{c'}\\), contradicting the definition of \\(r_{c'}\\).\n\nHence for the cell \\((r_c,c')\\) we have \\(c'\\ge\\pi(r_c)\\); therefore \\((r_c,c')\\notin A\\).  \nBut \\((r_c,c')\\) lies in the Cartesian product \\(I\\times J\\), i.e. inside the rectangle \\(R\\), contradicting the assumption \\(R\\subseteq A\\). ∎  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(B_A\\), at least \\(|B_A|=n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the number of rectangles in \\(B\\)\n\nThe argument for \\(B\\) is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\ns_c=\\min\\{\\,i\\mid \\pi(i)<c\\,\\},\n\\qquad\nt_c=(s_c,c).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\(t_c\\) belongs to \\(B\\) and is the **topmost** cell of \\(B\\) in column \\(c\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nB_B=\\{\\,t_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n\\,\\},\n\\qquad |B_B|=n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning as in Lemma 1 (with the inequalities reversed) shows that no rectangle contained in \\(B\\) can contain two distinct points of \\(B_B\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for region \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nEvery admissible tile lies wholly in \\(A\\) or wholly in \\(B\\).  \nHence the total number \\(t\\) of tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad B=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles for \\(A\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n*Tiles for \\(B\\).*  \nFor each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid c=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_c\\) consists of a single column and a contiguous block of rows, so it is a legal tile; similarly each \\(H_r\\) is a single row and a contiguous block of columns.  \nThe families \\(\\{V_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_r\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains an uncovered square \\((i,i)\\), and together they cover every cell of \\(A\\cup B\\).  \n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{V_c\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{H_r\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (11) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Final answer  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
