ncovered unit square’’ forces the uncovered squares to be a **permutation matrix**: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the unit square \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  \nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a product  \n\n\\[\nR\\times C\\qquad (R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\})\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) are **consecutive** sets of rows and columns, respectively.  \nThe tile covers precisely the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\).  \nBecause a tile may not contain an uncovered square, we must have  \n\n\\[\np(R)\\cap C=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling is a partition of the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}\n\\]\n\ninto rectangles \\(R_k\\times C_k\\) satisfying (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the two cells  \n\n\\[\nA_i=(i,p(i+1)),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,p(i)). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p(i+1)\\neq p(i)\\), both \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\); i.e. they are free squares.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{A_i,B_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(|S|=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Claim.**  \nNo axis‑aligned rectangle satisfying (1) can contain two distinct elements of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet a rectangle be \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) (interval notation for consecutive rows and columns).  \nAssume it contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).\n\n*Case 1.* The two cells are \\(A_i=(i,p(i+1))\\) and \\(B_i=(i+1,p(i))\\) for the same index \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(i,i+1\\in[a,b]\\) and \\(p(i),p(i+1)\\in[c,d]\\).  \nConsequently the rectangle also contains the cell \\((i+1,p(i+1))\\), which is a hole (the unco