ered unit square’’ forces the uncovered squares to be a **permutation matrix**: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the unit square \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  \nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently a tile is a product  \n\n\\[\nR\\times C\\qquad (R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\})\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) are **consecutive** sets of rows and columns, respectively.  \nThe tile covers precisely the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\).  \nBecause a tile may not contain an uncovered square, we must have  \n\n\\[\np(R)\\cap C=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling is a partition of the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F}=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}\n\\]\n\ninto rectangles \\(R_k\\times C_k\\) satisfying (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define the two cells  \n\n\\[\nA_i=(i,p(i+1)),\\qquad B_i=(i+1,p(i)). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p(i+1)\\neq p(i)\\), both \\(A_i\\) and \\(B_i\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal{F}\\); i.e. they are free squares.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{A_i,B_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(|S|=2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Claim.**  \nNo axis‑aligned rectangle satisfying (1) can contain two distinct elements of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet a rectangle be \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) (interval notation for consecutive rows and columns).  \nAssume it contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).\n\n*Case 1.* The two cells are \\(A_i=(i,p(i+1))\\) and \\(B_i=(i+1,p(i))\\) for the same index \\(i\\).  \nThen \\(i,i+1\\in[a,b]\\) and \\(p(i),p(i+1)\\in[c,d]\\).  \nConsequently the rectangle also contains the cell \\((i+1,p(i+1))\\), which is a hole (the uncovered square of row \\(i+1\\)).  \nThis contradicts (1).\n\n*Case 2.* The two cells are both of type \\(A\\), say \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nSince the rectangle contains rows \\(i\\) and \\(k\\), it also contains the intermediate row \\(i+1\\).  \nIts column interval contains both \\(p(i+1)\\) and \\(p(k+1)\\); therefore it contains the column \\(p(i+1)\\).  \nHence the rectangle contains the hole \\((i+1,p(i+1))\\), again contradicting (1).\n\n*Case 3.* The two cells are both of type \\(B\\); the argument is symmetric to Case 2.\n\nThus any rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\). ∎\n\nSince the tiles form a partition of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) and each tile can cover at most one element of \\(S\\), the number of tiles is at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering the part of row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\nan \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile covering the part of column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint; they cover every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) exactly once, and they avoid all diagonal cells.  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) and no other uncovered cell; similarly each column contains exactly one uncovered cell. Hence this constructi