"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Graph formulation.**  \n   Identify the uncovered unit squares with a permutation \\(p\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (\\(n=2025\\)): the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n   The set of covered squares is the edge\u2011set of the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{n,n}\\) with the perfect matching  \n   \\[\n   M=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n   \\]\n   removed.  \n   A rectangular tile corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph (a biclique) \\((R,C)\\) with the property  \n   \\[\n   C\\cap p(R)=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Inductive lower bound.**  \n   Let \\(f(n)\\) be the minimum number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \n   *Base case \\(n=2\\):* the two remaining squares are opposite corners; they cannot be covered by a single rectangle, so \\(f(2)=2=2\\cdot2-2\\).  \n   *Inductive step:* choose the row \\(r\\) whose uncovered square lies in the last column, i.e. \\(p(r)=n\\) (such a row exists because \\(p\\) is a permutation).  \n   - Any tile that contains a square of row \\(r\\) cannot contain column \\(n\\) (otherwise it would also contain the hole \\((r,n)\\)). Hence all squares of row \\(r\\) are covered by tiles that avoid column \\(n\\).  \n   - Symmetrically, all squares of column \\(n\\) are covered by tiles that avoid row \\(r\\).  \n   Consequently the tiling splits into three disjoint families:  \n   (i) tiles covering the \\(n-1\\) squares of row \\(r\\); at least one tile is needed.  \n   (ii) tiles covering the \\(n-1\\) squares of column \\(n\\); at least one tile is needed.  \n   (iii) tiles covering the remaining \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) sub\u2011board obtained by deleting row \\(r\\) and column \\(n\\).  \n   The uncovered squares of this sub\u2011board are exactly the restriction of the original permutation to the remaining rows and columns, so the sub\u2011board again satisfies the problem\u2019s condition. Hence it needs at least \\(f(n-1)\\) tiles.  \n   Therefore  \n   \\[\n   f(n)\\ge f(n-1)+2.\\tag{2}\n   \\]  \n   Using the induction hypothesis \\(f(n-1)=2(n-1)-2\\) and (2) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   f(n)\\ge\\bigl(2n-4\\bigr)+2=2n-2.\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \n   - For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n     \\]\n     an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n   The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint; together they cover every square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) and avoid all diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \n   The total number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (3) and the explicit construction both give the value \\(2n-2\\). Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles satisfying \u201ceach row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to be a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the unit square \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nAll other unit squares must be covered by tiles.  \n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; equivalently it is a product  \n\n\\[\n(R,C)\\qquad\\bigl(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\bigr)\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) are *consecutive* sets of rows and columns, respectively.  \nThe tile covers precisely the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a tile may not contain an uncovered square, we must have  \n\n\\[\nC\\cap p(R)=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus each tile corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph (a *biclique*) \\((R,C)\\) of the bipartite graph  \n\n\\[\nG_n=K_{n,n}\\setminus M,\n\\qquad \nM=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nand condition (1) is exactly the statement that the biclique avoids the edges of the perfect matching \\(M\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inductive lower bound\n\nDefine \\(f(n)\\) as the minimum number of tiles needed for an \\(n\\times n\\) board.\n\n**Base case \\(n=2\\).**  \nThe two uncovered squares are opposite corners; the remaining two squares cannot be covered by a single rectangle (any rectangle containing them would also contain a hole). Hence at least two tiles are necessary, and two \\(1\\times1\\) tiles suffice. Thus \\(f(2)=2=2\\cdot2-2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume \\(f(k)=2k-2\\) for all \\(k<n\\).  \nLet a tiling of the \\(n\\times n\\) board be given.  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, there exists a unique row \\(r\\) with \\(p(r)=n\\); the uncovered square of that row lies in the last column.\n\nConsider any tile that contains a square of row \\(r\\).  \nSince the tile cannot contain the hole \\((r,n)\\), its column interval must be contained in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently such a tile never contains a square of column \\(n\\).  \nThus **all** squares of row \\(r\\) are covered by tiles that avoid column \\(n\\).\n\nA symmetric argument shows that all squares of column \\(n\\) are covered by tiles that avoid row \\(r\\).\n\nHence the whole tiling can be partitioned into three disjoint families:\n\n1. tiles covering the \\(n-1\\) squares of row \\(r\\); at least one tile is needed,\n2. tiles covering the \\(n-1\\) squares of column \\(n\\); at least one tile is needed,\n3. tiles that avoid both row \\(r\\) and column \\(n\\).\n\nRemoving row \\(r\\) and column \\(n\\) leaves an \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) board.  \nThe uncovered squares that remain are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\neq r\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich constitute a permutation of the remaining rows and columns.  \nTherefore the third family is a tiling of a board of size \\(n-1\\) that satisfies the same condition, and it uses at least \\(f(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge f(n-1)+2.\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis \\(f(n-1)=2(n-1)-2\\); inserting this into (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge\\bigl(2n-4\\bigr)+2=2n-2.\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board requires at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nChoose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle.  \nFor each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{j+1,j+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.\n\n*Verification.*  \n- The tile \\(H_i\\) lies entirely in row \\(i\\) and in columns larger than \\(i\\); the uncovered square of row \\(i\\) is \\((i,i)\\), so \\(H_i\\) contains no uncovered square.  \n- The tile \\(V_j\\) lies entirely in column \\(j\\) and in rows larger than \\(j\\); the uncovered square of column \\(j\\) is \\((j,j)\\), so \\(V_j\\) also avoids uncovered squares.  \n- If \\(i<j\\) then the square \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(H_i\\); if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(V_j\\). Hence every off\u2011diagonal square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) is covered exactly once, and the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered.  \n- The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, so the tiles do not overlap.\n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{H_i\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{V_j\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nFrom the inductive lower bound (3) we have \\(f(n)\\ge 2n-2\\).  \nThe construction above shows \\(f(n)\\le 2n-2\\).  \nTherefore \\(f(n)=2n-2\\) for every \\(n\\ge2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer.**  \nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
