 holes.  \n\n7. Since the lower bound and the construction match, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(c_i\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \\sigma(i)=c_i\n\\]\n\nis a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Decomposition into left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered unit square belongs to exactly one of the two sets \\(L,R\\); the uncovered squares are precisely the points \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nA rectangle tile cannot contain an uncovered square, hence each tile is contained wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\).  \nConsequently the total number of tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\]\n\nWe treat the two regions separately.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) let  \n\n\\[\np_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1).\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\(p_i\\) is the **rightmost** covered cell in row \\(i\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, the columns \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\;( \\sigma(i)>1)\\) are all distinct; thus the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{p_i\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-1\\) cells, each lying in a different column.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\qquad\\text{No rectangle can contain two distinct cells of }\\mathcal P .\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells \\(p_i\\) and \\(p_k\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nLet the column interval of \\(R\\) be \\([a,b]\\) and the row interval be \\([r,s]\\); then  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\sigma(i)-1,\\;\\;b\\ge\\sigma(k)-1,\\qquad r\\le i,\\; s\\ge k .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(k)\\) then \\(\\sigma(i)\\in[a,b]\\) (because \\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(i)-1+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\ge a\\)).  \nSince row \\(i\\) lies in \\([r,s]\\), the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting that \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is uncovered.  \n\nIf \\(\\sigma(i)>\\sigma(k)\\) then \\(\\sigma(k)\\in[a,b]\\) (because \\(\\sigma(k)=\\sigma(k)-1+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(k)-1\\le b\\)).  \nRow \\(k\\) lies in \\([r,s]\\), so \\((k,\\sigma(k))\\in R\\), again a contradiction. ∎\n\nBecause each rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(\\mathcal P\\), at least \\(|\\mathcal P|=n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all cells of \\(L\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\ge n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nThe argument for \\(R\\) is symmetric.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) consider the leftmost covered cell  \n\n\\[\nq_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1).\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal Q:=\\{q_i\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}\\) also consists of \\(n-1\\) cells, all in distinct columns, and the same “no two in one rectangle’’ argument shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\). Thus\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\ge n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Global lower bound  \n\nAdding (1) and (2) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge 4048\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e., place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly below the diagonal)},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\quad\\text{(strictly above the diagonal)}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole left part of that row.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle covering the whole right part of that row.\n\nThe tiles \\(T_i^{\\text{left}}\\) and \\(T_i^{\\text{right}}\\) lie in distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint.  \nTogether they cover every cell of \\(L\\cup R\\); the only uncovered cells are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (3) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number of tiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
