 and any rectangle covering it must have rightmost column exactly \\(c(Q)\\).  \n\nNow for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consider the row \\(i_j=\\pi^{-1}(j+1)\\).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) lies in \\(L\\); by the claim any rectangle covering it has rightmost column \\(j\\).  \nSince a rectangle has a unique rightmost column, distinct values of \\(j\\) force distinct rectangles.  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Right region)\n\nDually, let \\(Q\\subseteq R\\) be a rectangle and define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell(Q)=\\min\\{j\\mid (i,j)\\in Q\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* There exists a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)=\\ell(Q)-1\\).  \n\n*Proof.* If every row \\(i\\) of \\(Q\\) satisfied \\(\\pi(i)\\le\\ell(Q)-2\\), then column \\(\\ell(Q)-1\\) would also lie to the right of all holes of those rows, so \\(Q\\) could be enlarged one column to the left, contradicting minimality of \\(\\ell(Q)\\). ∎  \n\nThus the cell \\((i,\\ell(Q))\\) with \\(\\pi(i)=\\ell(Q)-1\\) belongs to \\(R\\) and any rectangle covering it must have leftmost column \\(\\ell(Q)\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(i'_j=\\pi^{-1}(j-1)\\).  \nThe cell \\((i'_j,j)\\) lies in \\(R\\); any rectangle covering it has leftmost column \\(j\\).  \nAgain distinct values of \\(j\\) force distinct rectangles, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound\n\nFrom Lemmas 1 and 2 the total number of rectangles in any tiling satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n*Upper‑right region* (\\(R\\)): for each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Lower‑left region* (\\(L\\)): for each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nW_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe rectangles \\(V_j\\) and \\(W_j\\) are pairwise disjoint because in column \\(j\\) the two rectangles are separated by the uncovered cell \\((j,j)\\).  \nTogether they cover every non‑diagonal cell exactly once.  \nThe number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n| \\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}|+| \\{W_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Minimality\n\nInequality (1) gives a universal lower bound, and construction (2) shows that the bound is attainable.  \nTherefore the minimal possible number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board is exactly  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the required minimum number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
