n\n\\[\nD^{-}=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nA rectangle \\(R\\subseteq L\\) is determined by intervals of rows \\([a,b]\\) and columns \\([c,d]\\) with the property that every point of \\(R\\) satisfies \\(i>j\\). Consequently the smallest row index in \\(R\\) is \\(a\\) and the largest column index is \\(d\\); the condition \\(i>j\\) for all \\((i,j)\\in R\\) forces  \n\n\\[\na>d. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSuppose \\(R\\) contained two distinct squares \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((k,k-1)\\) from \\(D^{-}\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nThen \\(i\\) belongs to the row interval \\([a,b]\\) and \\(k-1\\) belongs to the column interval \\([c,d]\\).  \nHence \\(a\\le i\\) and \\(d\\ge k-1\\).  \nFrom \\(i<k\\) we obtain \\(i\\le k-1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le k-1\\le d,\n\\]\n\ncontradicting (1). ∎\n\nSince \\(|D^{-}|=n-1\\) and each rectangle of \\(L\\) can cover at most one element of \\(D^{-}\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2 (upper triangle).  \nAny rectangle contained in \\(U'\\) can contain at most one square of  \n\n\\[\nD^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma 1. A rectangle \\(R\\subseteq U'\\) satisfies \\(i<j\\) for all its points, which forces the largest row index to be smaller than the smallest column index. Repeating the reasoning with the squares \\((i,i+1)\\) yields the desired bound. ∎\n\nBecause \\(|D^{+}|=n-1\\), covering \\(U'\\) also needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Conclusion of the lower bound  \n\nThe two regions \\(L\\) and \\(U'\\) are disjoint, so a tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles. Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles  \n\nDefine, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Geometric description.*  \n\\(R_k\\) is the rectangle consisting of the first \\(k\\) columns and the rows strictly below them (rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\)).  \n\\(S_k\\) is the rectangle consisting of the first \\(k\\) rows and the columns strictly to their right (columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\)).\n\n**Properties.**\n\n1. **Disjointness.**  \nIf \\(k\\neq \\ell\\), the column sets of \\(R_k\\) and \\(R_\\ell\\) are \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\ell\\}\\) respectively, while their row sets are \\(\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\{\\ell+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\). The two rectangles intersect only when both the column and row intervals intersect, which would require a column index \\(\\le\\min(k,\\ell)\\) and a row index \\(\\ge\\max(k+1,\\ell+1)\\); such a pair always satisfies row > column, but the two rectangles lie on opposite sides of the diagonal and their row intervals are disjoint, so the intersection is empty. An analogous argument holds for the family \\(\\{S_k\\}\\). Moreover, every \\(R_k\\) lies entirely below the diagonal, while every \\(S_\\ell\\) lies entirely above it; hence no \\(R_k\\) meets any \\(S_\\ell\\).\n\n2. **Avoidance of diagonal squares.**  \nFor any \\((i,i)\\) on the diagonal we have \\(i\\le k\\) implies \\(i\\le k< i\\) is impossible, and \\(i\\ge k+1\\) implies \\(i\\ge k+1>k\\). Consequently \\((i,i)\\) never belongs to any \\(R_k\\) or \\(S_k\\).\n\n3. **Covering property.**  \nTake a non‑diagonal square \\((i,j)\\).  \nIf \\(i>j\\) (the square is in \\(L\\)), set \\(k=j\\). Then \\(j\\le k\\) and \\(i\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\).  \nIf \\(i<j\\) (the square is in \\(U'\\)), set \\(k=i\\). Then \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(j\\ge k+1\\), so \\((i,j)\\in S_k\\).  \nThus every off‑diagonal square belongs to exactly one of the rectangles.\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{R_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{S_k\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(2n-2\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles, none of which contains a diagonal square, and together they cover all squares of the board except the diagonal. Hence \\(\\mathcal T\\) is a valid tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Minimality  \n\nFrom (2) any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
