).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rect}(\\mathcal U)\\le N-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) and (3) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rect}(\\mathcal U)=N-1.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5. Lower and upper bounds for the lower‑triangular region  \n\nThe lower‑triangular region \\(\\mathcal L\\) is the transpose of \\(\\mathcal U\\).  \nApplying the same argument with the rightmost cells\n\\[\nb_i=(i,i-1)\\qquad (2\\le i\\le N)\n\\]\nshows that any rectangle contained in \\(\\mathcal L\\) can contain at most one of the \\(b_i\\).  \nHence at least \\(N-1\\) rectangles are necessary, and the horizontal strips\n\\[\nS_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<i\\}\\qquad (2\\le i\\le N)\n\\]\nprovide a partition with exactly \\(N-1\\) rectangles.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rect}(\\mathcal L)=N-1.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6. Minimal number of tiles for the whole board  \n\nEvery admissible tiling consists of a collection of rectangles covering \\(\\mathcal U\\) together with a collection covering \\(\\mathcal L\\); the two collections are disjoint.  \nConsequently the total number of tiles satisfies\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge \\operatorname{rect}(\\mathcal U)+\\operatorname{rect}(\\mathcal L)= (N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2.\n\\]\n\nThe construction described in Sections 3 and 5 uses exactly \\(2N-2\\) tiles:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{Vertical tiles }R_j\\;(2\\le j\\le N) \\text{ covering }\\mathcal U,\\\\\n&\\text{Horizontal tiles }S_i\\;(2\\le i\\le N) \\text{ covering }\\mathcal L.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), one per row and per column, as required.\n\nThus the lower bound is attainable, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2N-2\\).\n\nFor \\(N=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing your output, carefully review your \"Summary\" and \"Detailed Solution\" sections to ensure they are clean, rigorous, and strictly adhere to all instructions provided above. Verify that e