m) the covered squares form the single block  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le N-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(1<q(j)<N\\) the covered squares split into two non‑empty blocks  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<q(j)\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)<i\\le N\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle is a product \\(I\\times J\\) with \\(I\\) a set of consecutive rows; consequently a rectangle can cover at most **one** of the blocks (2)–(4) in a given column.  Hence\n\n* every column needs at least one rectangle;\n* every column whose hole is **not** in the top or bottom row needs at least two rectangles.\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the holes in the top row and in the bottom row lie in two distinct columns, namely \\(p(1)\\) and \\(p(N)\\).  Thus exactly two columns require only one rectangle, while the remaining \\(N-2\\) columns each require at least two.  Consequently any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2(N-2)+2=2N-2\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let \\(q(j)=p^{-1}(j)\\) be the row of its hole.  Define the two vertical strips\n\n\\[\nU_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<q(j)\\},\\qquad\nD_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)<i\\le N\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(q(j)=1\\) then \\(U_j=\\varnothing\\); if \\(q(j)=N\\) then \\(D_j=\\varnothing\\); otherwise both strips are non‑empty.  Each non‑empty strip is a rectangle of width 1 and height equal to the number of rows it contains.\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{U_j\\mid U_j\\neq\\varnothing\\}\\cup\\{D_j\\mid D_j\\neq\\varnothing\\}.\n\\]\n\n*All rectangles in \\(\\mathcal R\\) are pairwise disjoint*: they lie in different columns, and within a column the two strips are separated by the uncovered square \\((q(j),j)\\).  \n*Every covered square belongs to exactly one rectangle*: a covered square \\((i,j)\\) lies either above or below the hole in column \\(j\\), and therefore belongs to \\(U_j\\) or \\(D_j\\) respectively.  \n*The uncovered squares are precisely the holes*: the square \\((q(j),j)\\) is omitted from both \\(U_j\\) and \\(D_j\\), and no other square is omitted.\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal R\\) is a tiling of the board that satisfies the required condition.  Counting the rectangles, we obtain\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R| = 2(N-2)+2 = 2N-2,\n\\]\n\nbecause the two columns \\(p(1)\\) and \\(p(N)\\) contribute a single strip each, while each of the remaining \\(N-2\\) columns contributes two strips.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2N-2\\) rectangles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2N-2\\).  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2N-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(N=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2N-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nReview the “Summary” and “Detailed Solution” sections to confirm that every statement follows logically, all mathematical expressions are enclosed in TeX delimiters, and the final answer is presented in the required \\boxed{} format. Ensure that the lower‑bound argument correctly counts the two boundary columns and that the construction indeed yields exactly \\(2N-2\\) disjoint rectangles covering all squares except the prescribed holes. No extraneous commentary or incomplete reasoning should remain."
