lower bound\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\ge 2N-2}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define the vertical strips\n\\[\n\\mathcal U_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<q(j)\\}, &\\text{if }q(j)>1,\\\\[2pt]\n\\varnothing, &\\text{if }q(j)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal D_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{(i,j)\\mid q(j)<i\\le N\\}, &\\text{if }q(j)<N,\\\\[2pt]\n\\varnothing, &\\text{if }q(j)=N.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nEach non‑empty set \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) (resp. \\(\\mathcal D_j\\)) is a rectangle of width 1 and height equal to \\(|U_j|\\) (resp. \\(|D_j|\\)).  Collect all non‑empty strips:\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\bigl\\{\\mathcal U_j\\mid \\mathcal U_j\\neq\\varnothing\\bigr\\}\n          \\cup\n          \\bigl\\{\\mathcal D_j\\mid \\mathcal D_j\\neq\\varnothing\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nTwo strips belonging to different columns are disjoint because they occupy different columns.  Within the same column the two strips \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) and \\(\\mathcal D_j\\) are separated by the uncovered square \\((q(j),j)\\), so they are also disjoint.  Hence the rectangles in \\(\\mathcal R\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery covered square \\((i,j)\\) lies either above or below the hole in column \\(j\\); accordingly it belongs to \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) or \\(\\mathcal D_j\\).  Therefore the union of all rectangles in \\(\\mathcal R\\) equals the set of all covered squares, i.e. the whole board minus the uncovered squares \\(\\mathcal H\\).\n\n*Number of rectangles.*  \nFor the column whose hole is in the top row (\\(q(j)=1\\)) only \\(\\mathcal D_j\\) is non‑empty; for the column whose hole is in the bottom row (\\(q(j)=N\\)) only \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) is non‑empty.  All remaining \\(N-2\\) columns contribute both a non‑empty \\(\\mathcal U_j\\) and a non‑empty \\(\\mathcal D_j\\).  Hence\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R| = 2(N-2)+2 = 2N-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with e